# Advanced dependency injection

Most components should inject `Arc<T>` and let the container construct one shared value. Reach for the following tools only when the application's behavior requires selection, a different lifetime, a changing value, or resolution after construction.

## Choose one provider or use them all

Use a qualifier when a consumer needs one named implementation. Use a collection when each implementation is part of the work, such as a notification fan-out.

<Example title="Select one notifier and collect all notifiers">

```rust
use std::sync::Arc;
use upwell::prelude::*;

#[injectable]
trait Notifier: Send + Sync {
    fn send(&self, message: &str);
}
```

Expose the concrete components through the shared capability. Mark the natural default as `primary`.

```rust
#[component(provide = dyn Notifier, qualifier = "email", primary)]
struct EmailNotifier;

impl Notifier for EmailNotifier {
    fn send(&self, message: &str) {
        eprintln!("email: {message}");
    }
}

#[component(provide = dyn Notifier, qualifier = "log")]
struct LogNotifier;

impl Notifier for LogNotifier {
    fn send(&self, message: &str) {
        eprintln!("log: {message}");
    }
}
```

Select the provider needed for one operation, or inject every provider for a fan-out operation.

```rust
#[component]
struct Notifications {
    default: Arc<dyn Notifier>,
    #[qualifier = "log"]
    audit: Arc<dyn Notifier>,
    all: Vec<Arc<dyn Notifier>>,
}
```

</Example>

An unqualified single dependency needs one unambiguous provider. A qualifier is more precise than `primary`; it names the implementation the component expects. See [`#[component]`](/docs/latest/symbols/upwell_macros/component) and [`#[injectable]`](/docs/latest/symbols/upwell_macros/injectable) for provider declarations and ordering controls.

## Match the component lifetime to its state

Leave ordinary shared services at the default singleton lifetime. Use a protocol scope when state belongs to that connection or request, and use transient only when construction must happen for every resolution.

<Example title="Keep request state inside the request scope">

```rust
use std::sync::Arc;
use upwell::prelude::*;

struct PeerInfo;
```

Place state in the scope that owns it. A request component can use connection state because the connection outlives the request.

```rust
#[component(scope = upwell::daemon::Connection)]
struct Session {
    peer: PeerInfo,
}

#[component(scope = upwell::daemon::Request)]
struct RequestLog {
    session: Arc<Session>,
}
```

Construct per-resolution work as transient rather than storing it in a longer-lived component.

```rust
#[component(scope = transient)]
struct TraceId {
    #[default]
    id: uuid::Uuid,
}
```

</Example>

The important direction is from shorter-lived to equal- or longer-lived dependencies: a request may use a singleton, while a singleton must not capture a request. Redesign an invalid graph instead of using a shorter lifetime to hide it.

## Observe values that can change

An `Arc<T>` is the instance selected while the consumer is constructed. Use `Dep<T>` when component replacement should be visible later, and `Cfg<T>` when the component should read reloaded configuration.

<Example title="Read a live component and live configuration">

```rust
use upwell::prelude::*;

struct SearchIndex;

impl SearchIndex {
    fn query(&self, text: &str) -> Vec<String> {
        vec![text.into()]
    }
}

#[derive(serde::Deserialize)]
#[config(path = "http.limits")]
struct LimitConfig {
    max_body_bytes: usize,
}
```

Inject live handles only where later replacement is part of the component's contract.

```rust
#[component]
struct Search {
    index: Dep<SearchIndex>,
    limits: Cfg<LimitConfig>,
}
```

Read the current value when handling the operation rather than retaining it indefinitely.

```rust
impl Search {
    fn query(&self, text: &str) -> Vec<String> {
        let _maximum = self.limits.get().max_body_bytes;
        self.index.get().query(text)
    }
}
```

</Example>

Use `Dynamic<H>` instead when a protocol or framework supplies a value only at runtime. It is not a reload mechanism and does not make an otherwise missing component optional. The [`#[config]` symbol page](/docs/latest/symbols/upwell_macros/config) documents configuration declarations and bindings.

## Delay resolution for a real need

Use `Lazy<H>` when a valid dependency is expensive and used only on some paths. Use `Fresh<H>` when each operation needs a new factory-built value. Use `Deferred<T>` only when one side of a valid cycle is needed after construction.

<Example title="Create an expensive exporter only when it is used">

```rust
use std::sync::Arc;
use upwell::prelude::*;

struct Exporter;

impl Exporter {
    async fn write(&self, report: String) -> Result<(), AppError> {
        let _ = report;
        Ok(())
    }
}
```

Keep the delayed handle on the consumer.

```rust
#[component]
struct Reports {
    exporter: Lazy<Arc<Exporter>>,
}
```

Resolve it at the point where export work actually starts.

```rust
impl Reports {
    async fn export(&self, report: String) -> Result<(), AppError> {
        self.exporter.get_or_create().await?.write(report).await
    }
}
```

</Example>

Delayed handles preserve the consumer's scope and do not bypass dependency validation. They are not substitutes for an optional dependency or a provider qualifier. When a dependency is required during construction, inject it directly.

